{"id":"32035a37-7a9a-46cd-aae3-c5140b64d531","object":"chat.completion","created":1793351686,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Check `deepinfer/__init__.py` for import weight/circularity and look for existing tests about the new env var.\n\n```python\nprint(read_file('deepinfer/__init__.py')['content'][:2000])\nprint('----')\nprint(search_code('LOG_MAX_MESSAGE', ['deepinfer', 'tests'], 10))\n```","reasoning_content":"I need to implement the changes. Let me look at remaining details: logger.py needs `_truncate_message`. Also need `envs` import in logger.py. Let me check how environ is imported elsewhere in logger.py — currently not imported. Importing `deepinfer.environ` into logger.py: check for circular imports. environ.py imports os, threading only — safe.\n\nPlan:\nenviron.py: add helper factory method `int_or_none_min` or simply add to EnvVar class a static method `_int_min_or_none`. Requirement: \"描述符类可以新增辅助工厂方法（用于创建配置项的方法）\". So add static method:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_min(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{value}, must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nError message wraps with name: \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: ...\" via EnvVar.__get__. Good — includes variable name.\n\nThen in envs Logging section:\n```python\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_min(minimum=32)\n```\n\nNote empty string \"\" → int(\"\") raises ValueError → wrapped. Good.\n\nlogger.py:\n```python\nfrom deepinfer.environ import envs\n```\nCareful: deepinfer/__init__.py might import lots. Let's check whether `import deepinfer.environ` triggers heavy imports. Probably deepinfer/__init__.py is minimal but let's check.\n\nFormatter:\n\n```python\n_OMITTED_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n\n\ndef _truncate_message(message: str, max_message_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(message) <= max_message_chars:\n        return message\n    keep = max_message_chars - len(_OMITTED_MARKER)\n    head = (keep + 1) // 2\n    tail = keep - head\n    return message[:head] + _OMITTED_MARKER + (message[-tail:] if tail else \"\")\n```\n\nCheck example: budget 32, marker 15 chars (\"...<omitted>...\" = 3+9+3 = 15). keep = 17, head = 9, tail = 8. ✓ matches \"保留开头9字符、末尾8字符\".\n\nWait but requirement says \"标记计入预算\" and keep = budget - len(marker) = 17, head 9 tail 8 → total 9+15+8 = 32. ✓\n\nConstructor: 4 positional retained, `max_message_chars` keyword-only:\n\n```python\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        fmt: str,\n        datefmt: str | None = None,\n        high_precision: bool = False,\n        use_color: bool = False,\n        *,\n        max_message_chars: int | None = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int):\n                raise ValueError(...)\n            if max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n        super().__init__(fmt, datefmt)\n        ...\n        self.max_message_chars = max_message_chars\n```\n\nHmm — \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\". Should non-int (e.g. \"64\") raise ValueError? \"其他值须为大于等于32的整数，布尔值不算整数，否则抛出 ValueError\" — yes raise ValueError. Tests may check ValueError type for non-int. I'll raise ValueError with clear message mentioning max_message_chars.\n\nformat: \n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            body = record.message  # after args substitution\n            if len(body) > self.max_message_chars:\n                ...\n```\nCareful: record.message is set by super().format(). But super().format() computed msg = fmt % record.__dict__ using record.message. So we can truncate the body and re-render prefix. Better approach: before calling super().format(), compute the body separately and truncate, keeping record intact.\n\nPlan:\n```python\n    def format(self, record: logging.LogRecord) -> str:\n        truncated = None\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is not True:\n            body = record.getMessage()\n            if len(body) > self.max_message_chars:\n                truncated = _truncate_message(body, self.max_message_chars)\n                original_msg, original_args = record.msg, record.args\n                record.msg, record.args = truncated, None\n        try:\n            msg = super().format(record)\n        finally:\n            if truncated is not None:\n                record.msg, record.args = original_msg, original_args\n```\nWait: careful — mutating record.msg temporarily then restoring. Requirement: \"不能改写共享原记录的 msg、args、exc_info、stack_info\". Temporarily mutating and restoring is risky but final state is restored. Safer: use a copy? But exception formatting uses record.exc_info. Actually alternative: format full then replace the body. `record.message` is set by super().format(); prefix = msg[:msg.find(record.message)] — the existing code does this for multi-line. So we can:\n\n```python\n        msg = super().format(record)\n        if truncated is not None:\n            msg = msg.replace(record.message, truncated, 1)\n```\nBut record.message may appear... prefix computed before message so first occurrence is at prefix length. But if the message contains newlines, prefix alignment uses record.message length. Hmm, replace of record.message with truncated: msg = prefix + message + suffix(exception). Using slicing: idx = msg.find(record.message)? Since prefix is fixed format prefix which might contain the message text coincid