runcated is not None then record.msg restored. Good; exc_info untouched (super().format uses record.exc_info → full traceback preserved; but note: super().format in Formatter caches exc_text on the record — shared record's exc_text gets set, that's stock behavior anyway).\n\nHmm, but one issue: if exception info present, `super().format` uses record.exc_info → appends traceback. We keep it. Good.\n\nAlso `record.message` will be set to truncated body; subsequent handlers using the same record call format again which recomputes record.message = record.getMessage() with restored msg/args. Good, they see full.\n\nThen multiline prefix pass as before.\n\nNow `_setup_logger`: use envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS:\n```python\n    fmt = NewLineFormatter(\n        _FORMAT,\n        datefmt=_DATE_FORMAT,\n        use_color=sys.stdout.isatty(),\n        max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS,\n    )\n```\n\nBut logger.py importing environ: check for circular import — does environ import logger? No. But `deepinfer/__init__.py` may. Let me check quickly. Also check whether deepinfer.logger imported by environ? no. I'll import `from deepinfer.environ import envs` at module level in logger.py — but does deepinfer/__init__.py import engine stuff that imports logger... importing deepinfer.environ triggers `deepinfer/__init__.py` execution. If __init__ imports something that imports deepinfer.logger → circular. Safer: local import inside _setup_logger? But test might check module-level? Requirement doesn't say. I'll read deepinfer/__init__.py.\n\nRead __init__ and check the truncated helper needs. Also check test file maybe exists (tests/ dir) for expectations — reading tests is allowed (not modifying).\n\nLet's read deepinfer/__init__.py and search for existing tests on this feature."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":10115,"completion_tokens":2780,"total_tokens":12895,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":4096},"completion_to